catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ 03 — the Dictionary, top-down.
\
\ THE CLAIM (D01 §1, D-09, D-37): the Dictionary — the map from a name to what
\ it means — is itself an effect, `Dict`, ambient in every program and never
\ written down. So the words a program calls can be REBOUND from outside it,
\ with no cooperation from the program, no interface it had to implement, and
\ no indirection it had to pay for in advance.
\
\ Demo 02 needed the program to declare `!Log`. Nothing below needs anything.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ --- Ordinary code. Nothing here anticipates being reinterpreted.
catcat> define rate  ( -- i64 )        { 10 }
define tax   ( $amt:i64 -- i64 ) { $amt rate * 100 / }
define total ( $amt:i64 -- i64 ) { $amt $amt tax + }
defined rate ( -- i64 )
defined tax ( i64 -- i64 )
defined total ( i64 -- i64 )
catcat> 200 total show print "\n" print
220
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ (a) `with` — rebinding resolved at ELABORATION time.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ `with { old new } { body }` runs the body with `old` meaning `new`. It is
\ sugar for a `Dict` handler, discharged while the program is being built.
catcat> define eu_rate ( -- i64 ) { 20 }
defined eu_rate ( -- i64 )
catcat> define eu_total ( $amt:i64 -- i64 ) { with { rate eu_rate } { $amt total } }
defined eu_total ( i64 -- i64 )
catcat> 200 eu_total show print "\n" print
240
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ It reaches THROUGH `total` into `tax`, which is where `rate` is actually
\ called. That is not scope-based: `rate` is not a parameter of anything, and
\ the block does not lexically contain the call site.
catcat> \ And it costs nothing. `locate` decompiles the stored core term — there is no
\ source text kept — so this is the compiled program, and the `with` is gone:
catcat> locate eu_total
define eu_total ( i64 -- i64 ) {
  roll.0 pick.0 pick.1 roll.0 eu_rate * 100 / + roll.1 pop
}
catcat> \ How much further it inlines depends on definition order, and ONLY the
\ residual's size does, never the answer. The discharge pass walks the words
\ once from newest to oldest, so `eu_rate` — defined *after* the `rate` it
\ replaces — survives as a call. A replacement that is OLDER gets inlined in
\ its turn, and the whole thing folds to a constant:
catcat> define zero_rate ( -- i64 ) { 0 }
define rate2  ( -- i64 )         { 10 }
define tax2   ( $amt:i64 -- i64 ) { $amt rate2 * 100 / }
define duty_free ( $amt:i64 -- i64 ) { with { rate2 zero_rate } { $amt tax2 } }
defined zero_rate ( -- i64 )
defined rate2 ( -- i64 )
defined tax2 ( i64 -- i64 )
defined duty_free ( i64 -- i64 )
catcat> locate duty_free
define duty_free ( i64 -- i64 ) {
  roll.0 roll.0 0 * 100 /
}
catcat> \ --- The replacement must have the same STACK effect. Effects may differ
\ freely; that is the point of the construct.
catcat> define bad ( $amt:i64 -- i64 ) { with { rate not } { $amt total } }
error: with: not cannot replace rate; their signatures differ
catcat> \ --- Rebinding can REMOVE an effect from the type. `audited` is `!IO`;
\ swapping the noisy word for a quiet one makes the caller pure, and the
\ purity is in the signature, not a comment.
catcat> define log_rate ( -- i64 ) { "  rate looked up\n" print 10 }
define quiet_rate ( -- i64 ) { 10 }
defined log_rate ( -- i64 !IO )
defined quiet_rate ( -- i64 )
catcat> define audited ( $amt:i64 -- i64 ) { with { rate log_rate } { $amt total } }
define silent  ( $amt:i64 -- i64 ) { with { log_rate quiet_rate } { $amt audited } }
defined audited ( i64 -- i64 !IO )
defined silent ( i64 -- i64 )
catcat> 200 audited show print "\n" print
200 silent show print "\n" print
  rate looked up
220
220
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ (b) `handle Dict` — the same rebinding resolved at RUN time.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ Same construct, later. `with` is discharged during elaboration and costs
\ nothing; `handle Dict` is a frame consulted at the call and costs a lookup.
\ The ANSWER is the same, because they are one thing resolved at two times —
\ which is D-02, "compile-time specialization and runtime JIT are the same
\ operation", shown rather than asserted.
catcat> handle Dict over ( ) init { } { rate { eu_rate } } { 200 total } show print "\n" print
240
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ (c) A profiler, over code that knows nothing about profiling.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ A `Dict` handler may carry state, so it is a class over a WORD. Here the
\ state is a call counter and the replacement does the original work and then
\ bumps it. State sits on TOP, so the implementation is `( amt count -- result count )`.
catcat> define tax_impl ( $amt:i64 -- i64 ) { $amt rate * 100 / }
defined tax_impl ( i64 -- i64 )
catcat> "(c) profiling `total`, unmodified:\n  " print
handle Dict over ( i64 ) init { 0 } { tax { swap tax_impl swap 1 + } } { 200 total }
show print " call(s) to tax, total " print show print "\n" print
(c) profiling `total`, unmodified:
  1 call(s) to tax, total 220
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ Read that again: `tax` is an ORDINARY WORD, not an operation. It has no
\ effect in its signature, `total` was compiled against the real one, and the
\ handler still intercepted it. Instrumentation with no instrumentation.
catcat> \ The wrapper is a separate word on purpose. Calling `tax` from inside its own
\ replacement would reach the same frame while its state is lent out, which
\ the machine refuses (Q-12) — the same rule demo 02 showed for `Log`:
catcat> handle Dict over ( i64 ) init { 0 } { tax { swap tax swap 1 + } } { 200 total }
STUCK: handler re-entered while its own state is in use
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ (d) Mocking a dependency.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ The practical form of all of the above: replace what a program depends on,
\ at the call, without a seam having been designed in. `now` is not injected,
\ not a parameter, and not behind an interface.
catcat> define now ( -- i64 !IO ) { "  (asking the clock)\n" print 1000 }
define stamped ( $x:i64 -- i64 !IO ) { $x now + }
defined now ( -- i64 !IO )
defined stamped ( i64 -- i64 !IO )
catcat> define frozen ( -- i64 ) { 5 }
defined frozen ( -- i64 )
catcat> 7 stamped show print "\n" print
  (asking the clock)
1007
ok  (empty)
catcat> "(d) with a frozen clock: " print
handle Dict over ( ) init { } { now { frozen } } { 7 stamped } show print "\n" print
(d) with a frozen clock: 12
ok  (empty)
